WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:03.203
-Guys, today is Tuesday.

00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:06.039
And we're getting there,
you know?

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:08.808
This is a day-by-day thing.

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:12.679
And I think at this point,
everyone either knows someone

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:16.015
who knows someone
who has COVID-19

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:21.321
or knows someone who has had it
and beat it,

00:00:21.321 --> 00:00:25.158
or knows someone who's had it
and passed away.

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:29.796
Sadly for me,
today was my first passing.

00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:32.765
Our good friend Hal Willner
from "Saturday Night Live"

00:00:32.765 --> 00:00:37.069
who's a music producer, genius,
creative genius.

00:00:37.069 --> 00:00:39.339
I loved him so much.

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:42.208
Everyone loved him.
Every cast member loved him.

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:44.244
And we will miss him so much.

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:49.682
My thoughts go to his family
and Sheila and Arlo.

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:53.819
And, everyone, just take care
of each other and stay safe,

00:00:53.819 --> 00:00:57.256
and we're going to get through
this, you know, together.

00:00:57.256 --> 00:01:01.594
Let's talk and help
each other out whenever we can.

00:01:01.594 --> 00:01:04.964
I will talk more about how

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:08.934
as the -- as the weeks go on,
obviously.

00:01:08.934 --> 00:01:11.770
But that was --

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.539
It's kind of --

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:19.412
I'm happy that today our guests
on the show are friends of mine.

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:22.582
Justin Timberlake
is my guest tonight.

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:27.086
I've known him for
seems like forever.

00:01:27.086 --> 00:01:30.156 line:0%
Here is his charity
he's working with.

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:36.162 line:0%
It's gofundme.com/
americasfoodfund.

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:40.166 line:0%
That's where he's asking
everyone to donate.

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:44.703 line:0%
They're splitting their money
from Feeding America

00:01:44.703 --> 00:01:52.811 line:0%
and World Central Kitchen,

00:01:52.811 --> 00:01:54.947 line:0%
which is very --
They're both two amazing things,

00:01:54.947 --> 00:01:57.316 line:0%
which is also
what Russell Wilson and Ciara,

00:01:57.316 --> 00:02:00.920 line:0%
their charity is
Feeding America, as well.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.456 line:0%
And we have music
from Avett Brothers

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:10.696 line:0%
who want to give
to directrelief.org.

00:02:10.696 --> 00:02:13.799
Alright, everyone,
the show must go on.

00:02:13.799 --> 00:02:17.737
Let's start the show
with the monologue.

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:20.773 line:0%
♪♪

00:02:20.773 --> 00:02:22.542
Welcome to "The Tonight Show:
At Home Edition."

00:02:22.542 --> 00:02:24.244
Hey, here's some good news.

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:27.847
Today is National Beer Day.

00:02:27.847 --> 00:02:30.550
Americans celebrated
with national beer breakfast,

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:33.820
national beer lunch,
and national beer dinner.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.821
Did I ever tell you that story
about my dad

00:02:35.821 --> 00:02:37.022
when he was getting --

00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:39.925
My dad had prostate cancer.
He beat it.

00:02:39.925 --> 00:02:42.528
But he was getting checked out,

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:44.763
and he was like --
wanted to stay local.

00:02:44.763 --> 00:02:46.865
We're from upstate New York,
and he was like --

00:02:46.865 --> 00:02:49.735
Did I ever tell you this?
-I don't think so.

00:02:49.735 --> 00:02:53.773
-If you know my dad,
he used to drink a lot of beer.

00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:56.475
Anyway, he was like, "Yeah,
I'm going to get checked out."

00:02:56.475 --> 00:02:58.344
I go, "Yeah, absolutely."
He's like, "It's a good doctor."

00:02:58.344 --> 00:02:59.678
I go, "Are you sure he's good?

00:02:59.678 --> 00:03:01.013
Have you checked the
references?" He's like, "Yeah."

00:03:01.013 --> 00:03:02.882
I go, "Alright, what's his name
so I can check him out?"

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:05.885
He goes, "His name is, uh,
Dr. Beer."

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:08.421
-[ Laughs ]
-I go...

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:10.022
"Dad."

00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:14.026
If irony -- If irony exists --

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:17.629
"You can't -- There is no way
you can let Dr. Beer kill you.

00:03:17.629 --> 00:03:19.465
We're not letting that happen."

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:22.435
He was probably a great doctor,
but we did not see --

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:24.604
I don't believe we saw Dr. Beer.

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:28.074
Dr. Beer he was going to go see.

00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:29.742
[ Both laugh ]

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:31.811
Oh, my goodness.

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:34.814
Love you, Dad.
I know you're watching this.

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:37.984
And he probably did go see
Dr. Beer and didn't tell me.

00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:43.556
Maybe he saw his son
[Laughing] Dr. Light Beer.

00:03:46.592 --> 00:03:48.127
Guys, after 11 seasons,

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:50.963
tomorrow is the series finale
of "Modern Family."

00:03:50.963 --> 00:03:52.765
That's right.
They were fine for 11 years.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:55.701
Then after just four weeks
of quarantine, it's over.

00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:57.770
That's what it takes.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:00.406
Well, this isn't good.
The CEO of SeaWorld has resigned

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:02.641
after just five months
on the job.

00:04:02.641 --> 00:04:04.544
Even crazier,
today he was charged

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:07.513
with hiring a hit man
to take out Carole Baskin.

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:11.083
Gosh. Everyone's got it out
for poor Carole.

00:04:11.083 --> 00:04:12.451
Some entertainment news.

00:04:12.451 --> 00:04:15.888
There's a new show on Quibi
called "Murder House Flip"

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:19.792
where people renovate
notorious homicide homes.

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:21.493
But I'm more excited
for the spin-off show,

00:04:21.493 --> 00:04:23.630
"Tiny Murder House Flip."

00:04:27.366 --> 00:04:29.869
Some sports news.
UFC President Dana White said

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:33.773
he's securing a private island
so fights can continue.

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:36.609
Yeah, people fighting on a
private island, or as I call it,

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:40.847
every movie starring
Jean Claude Van Damme.

00:04:40.847 --> 00:04:42.381
I saw a lot of people
are now calling for

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:45.818
the S.A.T. to be
completely online.

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:47.920
If this happens,
testing boards are asking kids

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:52.858
to make sure they use
a #2 computer.

00:04:52.858 --> 00:04:54.360
During quarantine,
many Americans

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.962
have been studying
their family genealogy.

00:04:56.962 --> 00:04:59.698
I guess after spending a month
together, everyone's thinking,

00:04:59.698 --> 00:05:01.167
"Am I really related
to these people?"

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:04.470
-[ Chuckles ]

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:06.572
-I read that some people
are taking this time at home

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:08.841
to learn an instrument.

00:05:08.841 --> 00:05:10.776
Yeah, 'cause being quarantined
with your family

00:05:10.776 --> 00:05:12.611
wasn't stressful enough.

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:14.981
Let's throw in learning
an instrument into the mix.

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:18.283
[ Off-key violin ]
You sound great, honey.

00:05:18.283 --> 00:05:20.219
Yeah. Always wanted
to learn the violin.

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:23.555
Is that right?

00:05:23.555 --> 00:05:24.991
I don't know about this.

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:28.995
There's now an online recipe
for Peeps-infused vodka.

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:31.830
It's fun, except for the three
hours you got to listen

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.899
to a marshmallow bunny
keep asking,

00:05:33.899 --> 00:05:37.170
[Slurring] "Do you think
I'm a pretty Peep?"

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:43.275
And finally, a triathlete was
competing in a virtual Ironman

00:05:43.275 --> 00:05:47.279
when she said her idiot husband
tripped over the power cord.

00:05:47.279 --> 00:05:48.781
If you want to hear more
on this story,

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:52.351
tune in to next week's episode
of "Murder House Flip."

00:05:52.351 --> 00:05:55.621
There you go, everyone.
That is my monologue.

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:57.623
We have a great show.
As I mentioned,

00:05:57.623 --> 00:05:59.792
from the new
"Trolls World Tour" movie,

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:01.860
Justin Timberlake is here.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.329
We also have Russell Wilson,
superstar quarterback,

00:06:04.329 --> 00:06:06.766
and Ciara, superstar singer,
on the show,

00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:08.934
and music by the Avett Brothers.

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:12.338
Now it is time
for #AskTheFallons.

00:06:12.338 --> 00:06:17.276 line:0%
♪♪

00:06:17.276 --> 00:06:21.013
Look like Michael Myers
from "Halloween."

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:22.948
Like the mask --
I look like the mask he wears.

00:06:22.948 --> 00:06:25.684
-And I look like Jamie Lee
Curtis from "Halloween."

00:06:25.684 --> 00:06:27.819
-Honey.
-Honey.

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:30.556
-Jamie Lee Curtis is beautiful.
You're beautiful.

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:32.692
-[ Laughing ]

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:36.229
-Okay. Alright.

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:38.998
Welcome back to --
Are you changing again?

00:06:38.998 --> 00:06:40.233
Is there something else
following us?

00:06:40.233 --> 00:06:41.500
Oh, it's a sign.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:42.601
Thought it was an animal.

00:06:42.601 --> 00:06:43.769
[ Both laugh ]

00:06:43.769 --> 00:06:45.371
-Oh, Lord.
-Thought there was an animal,

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:47.272
a white animal following us.

00:06:47.272 --> 00:06:50.242
-[ Laughs ] Scary.
-Alright.

00:06:50.242 --> 00:06:54.113
-So, we have good news.

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:56.215
Winnie lost a tooth today.

00:06:56.215 --> 00:06:58.250
-Yes. You'll see it, though,
in the interview.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.552
-Oh -- Oh, rats. I forgot
we interrupt you in the middle.

00:07:00.552 --> 00:07:02.488
-Spoiler alert.
-Sorry.

00:07:04.657 --> 00:07:06.525
-This is from @--

00:07:06.525 --> 00:07:08.694
These are all different
questions people are saying.

00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:11.330
#AskTheFallons.
-Okay.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.634
-A lot of them are just stuff
like, "What do you eat?"

00:07:14.634 --> 00:07:16.668
and all this stuff that you --
You wouldn't care.

00:07:16.668 --> 00:07:18.538
-Olives. I'm obsessed
with olives.

00:07:18.538 --> 00:07:21.039
-Well, I mean -- Yes.
[ Both laugh ]

00:07:21.039 --> 00:07:23.041
Alright, well --
-Jalapeño-stuffed olives.

00:07:23.041 --> 00:07:24.644
Mmm!
-You love olives.

00:07:24.644 --> 00:07:25.744
-Yeah.

00:07:25.744 --> 00:07:27.546
-How did you start
with Graber olives?

00:07:27.546 --> 00:07:32.150
-Grabers have been in our family
for a hundred years.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:36.055
It's a great place
out of mid-California,

00:07:36.055 --> 00:07:38.524
the "Graver" Olive Company.

00:07:38.524 --> 00:07:40.525
And they are delicious.
-The Graber olive --

00:07:40.525 --> 00:07:42.829
-Graber --

00:07:42.829 --> 00:07:45.665
-"It's the Graver Olive Company,
and they make Graver olives."

00:07:45.665 --> 00:07:47.833
-I just meant to say Graber.

00:07:47.833 --> 00:07:51.036
Graber --
Anyway, the best olives!

00:07:51.036 --> 00:07:53.672
-Alright, this one's from @--

00:07:53.672 --> 00:07:57.342
This one's from @Jaco1948Jaco.

00:07:57.342 --> 00:07:59.478
It says, "Who did
all that amazing artwork

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:01.246
on the walls of your house?"

00:08:01.246 --> 00:08:03.081
I'll leave -- That's definitely
a question for you.

00:08:03.081 --> 00:08:06.319
-Yes. However,
who did it is hilarious,

00:08:06.319 --> 00:08:08.086
unless they're speaking
about one thing,

00:08:08.086 --> 00:08:13.158
because it is a lifetime
of collecting heirlooms,

00:08:13.158 --> 00:08:16.796
like, my grandparents' stuff,
my dad's stuff, my mom's stuff,

00:08:16.796 --> 00:08:19.631
and then a lot
of flea market digging

00:08:19.631 --> 00:08:24.203
and tiny little shops in New
Hampshire and the Hudson Valley,

00:08:24.203 --> 00:08:27.906
and Palm Springs has got
amazing goody thrift shops,

00:08:27.906 --> 00:08:30.242
and nothing's antique --

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:33.112
Oh, eBay. Tons on eBay.

00:08:33.112 --> 00:08:35.481
I really love finding great --

00:08:35.481 --> 00:08:37.717
old paintings,
vintage paintings.

00:08:37.717 --> 00:08:43.122
And our style -- I say "ours"
'cause it is ours now, is --

00:08:43.122 --> 00:08:46.024
-Is anything of mine
hanging on the walls?

00:08:46.024 --> 00:08:47.192
-A little.

00:08:47.192 --> 00:08:48.861
Is, like, original --

00:08:48.861 --> 00:08:50.996
The sailing, the sailing room,
that whole --

00:08:50.996 --> 00:08:52.731
-That's true. Yeah, yeah.
I do have some ship paintings.

00:08:52.731 --> 00:08:54.200
-Ship paintings. But I love it.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:55.934
-We don't have
any famous paintings.

00:08:55.934 --> 00:08:57.169
-No. Anyone --

00:08:57.169 --> 00:08:59.205
Yeah. If you came in
to steal our artwork,

00:08:59.205 --> 00:09:03.008
you'd have to steal like 2,000
paintings to add up to anything,

00:09:03.008 --> 00:09:05.144
and that would be
so hard to steal.

00:09:05.144 --> 00:09:08.247
Yeah, we don't actually
have much of value.

00:09:08.247 --> 00:09:10.649
We just have
things from the heart,

00:09:10.649 --> 00:09:11.917
things we love.

00:09:11.917 --> 00:09:14.987
-Yeah, which is...valuable.
-Yeah.

00:09:14.987 --> 00:09:17.657
-Which is invaluable.
-Invaluable.

00:09:17.657 --> 00:09:21.360
Um...

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:26.398
Um, alright, This one is from

00:09:26.398 --> 00:09:29.768
@LivingInFallony.

00:09:29.768 --> 00:09:32.871
@LivingInFallony.

00:09:32.871 --> 00:09:35.408
"What's the story behind getting
a slide in your house?"

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:37.276
Everyone's asking
about the slide.

00:09:37.276 --> 00:09:38.944
Is there a real story?
-Yeah.

00:09:38.944 --> 00:09:45.518
Well, the slide is in
an old barn,

00:09:45.518 --> 00:09:49.889
and the only thing

00:09:49.889 --> 00:09:52.491
I knew that we wanted to do
with the barn

00:09:52.491 --> 00:09:54.560
was put a slide
down the middle of it,

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:56.829
and I have a very curious --
-Did I know that?

00:09:56.829 --> 00:09:58.864
-Yeah. Remember that drawing?

00:09:58.864 --> 00:10:01.066
It didn't come out
quite as cute as it should have.

00:10:01.066 --> 00:10:03.769
-Usually, things start
with a drawing on an index card.

00:10:03.769 --> 00:10:05.237
-Yeah, everything's
on index cards.

00:10:05.237 --> 00:10:06.404
No lines.
-With that black pen --

00:10:06.404 --> 00:10:07.773
that black ink.

00:10:07.773 --> 00:10:09.942
-Black flair pen.
-Yeah.

00:10:09.942 --> 00:10:14.647
-And I drew that slide
coming down.

00:10:14.647 --> 00:10:15.914
And then --

00:10:15.914 --> 00:10:19.117
Once you draw it, I think
you can bring it to life.

00:10:19.117 --> 00:10:23.723
So the point of it was because
how cool and fun is that?

00:10:23.723 --> 00:10:25.224
I don't know. It just is so fun.

00:10:25.224 --> 00:10:27.526
I go down that slide
usually all the time.

00:10:27.526 --> 00:10:31.063
I go down with tons of laundry.
[ Laughs ]

00:10:31.063 --> 00:10:33.365
I go down with anything.
And I use the slide --

00:10:33.365 --> 00:10:34.800
-Kids, adults,
everyone uses the slide.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.202
-Shoot my way out and then,
yeah, walk up the stairs.

00:10:37.202 --> 00:10:39.304
-But do you remember the first
time, when we were dating,

00:10:39.304 --> 00:10:41.106
and you saw my apartment,

00:10:41.106 --> 00:10:42.875
and you were like, "Wow,
this is cool. What's that?"

00:10:42.875 --> 00:10:46.611
And I go, "I want that
to be a secret room."

00:10:46.611 --> 00:10:48.380
-Yes.
-Like, behind the wall.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.217
And you're like, "Yeah. Why?"

00:10:51.217 --> 00:10:52.718
-I did? No.

00:10:52.718 --> 00:10:56.188
Oh, that --
You had, like, a closet

00:10:56.188 --> 00:10:57.790
or a pantry or something.
-No, it was like --

00:10:57.790 --> 00:11:00.258
The pantry --
It's now where the pantry is,

00:11:00.258 --> 00:11:02.093
but at one point,
I had something designed

00:11:02.093 --> 00:11:04.763
where if you sit on the chair,
the wall spins.

00:11:04.763 --> 00:11:06.398
-Yes.
-And it's like a James Bond.

00:11:06.398 --> 00:11:08.400
-I'm surprised I said why.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:10.369
-Well, it was like,
"What are you hiding from?"

00:11:10.369 --> 00:11:12.471
Like, "Why would you need
a hiding place?"

00:11:12.471 --> 00:11:14.874
-Well, I'm sorry, old self,
for saying that,

00:11:14.874 --> 00:11:18.410
in former form
before we were married you.

00:11:18.410 --> 00:11:22.014
-Then I had another idea
to have a door

00:11:22.014 --> 00:11:25.151
that's in the cabinet of under
our sink in the bathroom

00:11:25.151 --> 00:11:26.919
that you can go
through the cabinet

00:11:26.919 --> 00:11:29.722
and come out the other bedroom
and then go to --

00:11:29.722 --> 00:11:31.423
and then, like,
leave the apartment.

00:11:31.423 --> 00:11:34.326
-I want to say, like, "Why?"
but I would totally do it.

00:11:34.326 --> 00:11:35.494
So I'm in.

00:11:35.494 --> 00:11:36.929
-Yeah, I have no idea why.

00:11:36.929 --> 00:11:38.397
But I'm glad we didn't.
-I like it.

00:11:38.397 --> 00:11:39.765
-But we did put, like,
a secret bookcase

00:11:39.765 --> 00:11:41.633
for the play room for the kids.
-Yes.

00:11:41.633 --> 00:11:43.002
-And we have a slide now.

00:11:43.002 --> 00:11:45.137
-Yes.

00:11:45.137 --> 00:11:47.305
-There you go.
-Yes. Hi.

00:11:47.305 --> 00:11:48.640
-Hi, guys.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:50.542
Ooh, new shades.
Trying them out?

00:11:50.542 --> 00:11:52.410
They look good, bud.
-Aww, cute.

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:53.712
-Any other questions?

00:11:53.712 --> 00:11:56.614
-I was thinking maybe I should
interview you next week.

00:11:56.614 --> 00:11:57.782
-Alright. Here we go.

00:11:57.782 --> 00:11:58.884
-Bye!
-Back to the show.

00:11:58.884 --> 00:12:00.052
-Bye, everyone. Ha, ha.

